window.docContents[98].resolve({"/IEC-80000-6-___-Electromagnetism/Dimension-does-not-classify_SEMI_-the-kind-does-___-the-power-and-impedance-cases/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-6-___-Electromagnetism--Dimension-does-not-classify_SEMI_-the-kind-does-___-the-power-and-impedance-cases":{"contents":"Part 6 holds the sharpest dimension collision in the series so far. Active power (6-56),\nreactive power (6-60), and apparent power (6-57) are all M·L²·T⁻³, yet they are\ndistinct kinds — and the collision is sharper than Part 5's joule-per-kelvin, because there\neven the unit string agreed, while here the standard itself spends three strings (W,\nvar, VA) on the one dimension. Resistance (6-46) and reactance (6-51.4) collide the\nsame way at the ohm — the real and imaginary parts of an impedance — and the relative\npermittivity (6-15), relative permeability (6-27), the susceptibilities, the coupling and\nleakage factors, and the quality, loss, and power factors fill out the dimension-one family.\n\n\n\nActive power is not reactive power, though both are M·L²·T⁻³. There exist distinct\nIEC 80000-6 kinds with the same dimension — active and reactive power witness it, alongside\napparent, complex, and non-active power. No dimension-only type system can separate them;\nthe kind layer does. This is the electromagnetic case that a dimension does not classify a\nquantity, sharper than Part 5's. Uses the dimension map.\n\niec80000_6_dim_collision : ∃ a b : DimensionedKind, a.kind ≠ b.kind ∧ a.dim = b.dim,\nwitnessed by ⟨activePower, reactivePower, …⟩ with activePower_ne_reactivePower and\nreflexivity. The companion resistance_ne_reactance is the ohm case (real vs imaginary\npart of an impedance). Axiom-free.\n\nThe active-power watt is not the reactive-power var. The units of active and reactive\npower carry the same dimension M·L²·T⁻³, yet are not commensurable — and the standard\nkeeps them apart not only by kind but by string, W versus var, a sharper form of the\nrule that neither a dimension nor a unit string determines a kind. Uses\nmetrological units.\n\nwattActive_varReactive_not_commensurable : ¬ wattActive.Commensurable varReactive by\nunfolding to the underlying kinds and decide (active power's kind differs from the\nreactive-power kind; the units are written \"W\" and \"var\").\n\nThe dimension-1 disambiguation, on the standard. There exist distinct IEC 80000-6 kinds\nwith the same dimension one — relative permittivity and relative permeability witness it,\nalongside the susceptibilities, the coupling and leakage factors, and the quality, loss,\nand power factors. Dimension cannot separate them; the kind layer does. Uses\nthe dimension map.\n\niec80000_6_dim_one_collision : ∃ a b : DimensionedKind, a.kind ≠ b.kind ∧ a.dim = b.dim ∧\na.dim = 1, witnessed by ⟨relativePermittivity, relativePermeability, …⟩ with\nrelativePermittivity_ne_relativePermeability (a decide) and reflexivity. Axiom-free.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009IEC 80000-6 — Electromagnetism","header":"24.4. Dimension does not classify; the kind does — the power and impedance cases","id":"/IEC-80000-6-___-Electromagnetism/Dimension-does-not-classify_SEMI_-the-kind-does-___-the-power-and-impedance-cases/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-6-___-Electromagnetism--Dimension-does-not-classify_SEMI_-the-kind-does-___-the-power-and-impedance-cases"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L1-___-Two-bodies/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Synopsis--PKC-vocabulary-in-scope":{"contents":"New at this step:\n\n* SortOfSystem — the universal category of a system, here \"harmonic oscillator\".* Sorted (sortOf) — an object type whose objects know their sort.* Designated (designation, designation_inj) — an object type whose objects have distinct nominal names.* IndividualQuantity — a magnitude of kind k about one object o.* IndividualQuantity.add, IndividualQuantity.div — same-object, kind-licensed arithmetic.* DifferenceKind.ofScale — the difference law that licenses comparing two records of one quantity.* massK, springConstantK, omegaSquaredK — the oscillator's kinds; k/m has kind omegaSquaredK.\n\nCarried from earlier steps: Quantity, massQ, springQ.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L1 — Two bodies\u0009Synopsis","header":"5.2.1.2. PKC vocabulary in scope","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L1-___-Two-bodies/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Synopsis--PKC-vocabulary-in-scope"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L4-___-Out-of-the-line/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L4-___-Out-of-the-line--Learning-path":{"contents":"* #* Do* Checked by HCP.L4_OutOfTheLine anchor* Concept disclosed\n* 1* Put the two centres in a PairState3; read position, displacement, and distance, then try the two kind collisions.* points_vectors_and_distances* Position and displacement are distinct vector kinds; distance and the length of displacement are distinct scalar kinds, even where magnitudes agree. HCP's body and separation bearers retain nominal identity.\n* 2* Attach the distance to the named pair, compare two states, and mix P with Q.* whose_distance* distance admits DifferenceKind.ofScale; same-name states have a typed change whose magnitude reads their own centres, while Q's separation cannot be added to P's.\n* 3* Relate distance to displacement length and apply the triangle inequality to state points.* distance_is_length* distanceQ_eq_lengthOf_displacementQ and distanceQ_triangle operate directly on the pair state's centres.\n* 4* Feed one state-derived bare frame vector to all three upstream transports and add the cart's origin velocity.* bare_frames* Both terms compile, but are physically wrong when the vector's role is displacement: bare F.Vector cannot select a transport law or distinguish displacement from velocity.\n* 5* Use KVector for displacement and velocity; try the wrong boost and velocity-plus-force.* kinded_frames* Frame components enter through each frame's own dispEquiv; the observer-independence theorem verifies geometric transport. Boost-on-displacement and velocity-plus-force are pinned refusals. Average velocity is computed from two same-pair states and an elapsed duration.\n* 6* State the cart-origin displacement law and try adding its frame velocity to the pair displacement.* observer_motion* origin_displacement_eq makes observer motion an attribute of the cart frame; the kinded displacement-plus-velocity sum is refused.\n* 7* Map Space Stages 0–4 and exercise Stage 3's operator table.* adoption_ladder* spanArea and its magnitude law are accepted on a displacement from the state and a probe point; area × area is refused. Stage 4's boundary and API-map audits are the final rung.\n\nWorksheet: HCP/Worksheets/L4_OutOfTheLine.lean · Solution: HCP/L4_OutOfTheLine.lean\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L4 — Out of the line","header":"5.5.6. Learning path","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L4-___-Out-of-the-line/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L4-___-Out-of-the-line--Learning-path"},"/The-Proved-Spine/Specialization-and-mutual-comparability-_LPAR_OML-___-Dybk___r-___6_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--The-Proved-Spine--Specialization-and-mutual-comparability-_LPAR_OML-___-Dybk___r-___6_RPAR_":{"contents":"\n\nSpecialization Specializes E is the reflexive–transitive closure of a\ndirect-parent edge relation E over kind-of-property. Different E give different per-application hierarchies — the extensibility a\nfixed inductive would deny.\n\nAn inductive with refl and step constructors; of_edge lifts a single\nedge.\n\nSpecialization is a preorder. It is reflexive (the refl constructor) and\ntransitive (Specializes.trans). Stating — let alone proving — an algebraic law\nof the taxonomy is exactly what an OWL2 reasoner cannot do; it checks\nconsistency, not laws. Uses specialization.\n\nTransitivity is by induction on the first derivation: refl returns the second\nderivation, step re-applies the constructor under the inductive hypothesis.\n\nTwo kinds are mutually comparable iff they share a common super-kind\n(). This is how width and\nheight stay comparable as lengths while remaining distinct sub-kinds. Uses specialization.\n\nAn existential over a shared upper bound.\n\nMutual comparability is reflexive (refl) and symmetric (symm), and any\nspecialization induces it (of_specializes). Uses mutual comparability.\n\nReflexivity takes the witness a itself; symmetry swaps the two halves of the\nconjunction.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009The Proved Spine","header":"6.3. Specialization and mutual comparability (OML / Dybkær §6)","id":"/The-Proved-Spine/Specialization-and-mutual-comparability-_LPAR_OML-___-Dybk___r-___6_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--The-Proved-Spine--Specialization-and-mutual-comparability-_LPAR_OML-___-Dybk___r-___6_RPAR_"},"/Write-Once___-Correctly/The-five-steps/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--The-five-steps--Step-1-___-Start-from-the-measurement-principle___-declare-the-kinds":{"contents":"Before any number, name what each quantity is by the examination that produces it. A\nkind-of-property is the common defining aspect of mutually comparable\nproperties (Dybkær §6.19); two otherwise-comparable kinds are individuated by their\nexamination principle — the defining aspect of the procedure that\nmeasures them, so that a different principle forces a different\nkind. This is the layer a description logic partly has: it can record a class, but not the\noperator algebra or the laws the later steps add.In the example this is the catalogue of bare kinds — the radar backscatter , the\noptical ndvi, the surface reflectivity, and the Mironov dielectric fit-constant kinds — each\ndeclared as a def of KindOfProperty, named by the instrument or principle that examines it.\nThat a different examination principle forces a different kind is itself checked there: monostatic\nand bistatic reflectivity share name, scale, and dimension yet are distinct kinds. A kind is\ngeneral (a type); this pixel's backscatter is individual (a term, Quantity backscatter α).\nFix the kinds first: everything above is indexed by them.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Write Once, Correctly\u0009The five steps","header":"18.1.1. Step 1 — Start from the measurement principle: declare the kinds","id":"/Write-Once___-Correctly/The-five-steps/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--The-five-steps--Step-1-___-Start-from-the-measurement-principle___-declare-the-kinds"}});